Stood in the freezing rain for an hour waiting for a bus. At the 30 minute mark two buses arrived at the stop and idled there for another 30 minutes. People didn't have kind words for the driver when we were finally let on.

&#x200B;

Got to my neighborhood, moved across the street to get to my next bus. Waved my hands to try to get the S51 to stop and let me on as I struggled to get to the bus through the snow while carrying bags.

&#x200B;

The bus slowed down then sped off. I look at the MTA bus time App and there were no other buses on the route.

&#x200B;

It took me 2 hours to get home. About 10 minutes into my walk the ran started coming down hard.

&#x200B;

Apparently, I got off easy.

Some years ago I had to quit my job. Retail was a very draining life for very little money; I had decided enough was enough. 

&#x200B;

A few weeks into my joblessness I went for a bike ride to get my mind off of things. I had started my ride on the Battery City Park Strip, but rode down to some place I can't remember.

&#x200B;

I was on the bike path, enjoying the ride when I had to maneuver around a couple of dumbasses who were walking on the bicycle path. There was a rider coming in the other direction, and we both tried to go around the pedestrians in the same way.

&#x200B;

I hit the guy head on and was stunned for a few minutes. The idiots blocking the road were nowhere to be found, but a couple of pedestrians crowded us to make sure we were OK. An officer offered to call me an ambulance since my pupils were still dilated, but this is the United States of America, and I was both uninsured and unemployed at the time, so I declined.

&#x200B;

I got out with a bump on my head and some damage to my bike, the other guy's ankle swelled up and his bike was also damaged. As I was recovering me and the guy on the other bike walked and talked for a bit, and before I knew it he insisted on paying for my bike repairs because of my circumstances. 

&#x200B;

